    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Pulmonary tuberculosis, intensive phase (A15.0)</content>. Good early response with sputum smear conversion. Liver tests normal (ALT 34, AST 31, bilirubin 0.8). No isoniazid or fluoroquinolone resistance on molecular testing; phenotypic susceptibility pending. [S]<list><item>Continue the four drugs and pyridoxine daily by video with breakfast.</item><item>Monthly sputum culture: the samples from the third week are the first.</item><item>Back to teaching with the health department&apos;s agreement; no mask is needed now.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Cystic fibrosis and alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency</content>. Sweat chloride 18 mmol/L, below 30, so cystic fibrosis is unlikely; alpha-1 antitrypsin 142 mg/dL, normal, so severe deficiency is excluded. With the CT showing no bronchiectasis or emphysema, neither explains his illness; tuberculosis does. [S]<list><item>No further testing for either.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Moderate protein-calorie malnutrition (E44.0)</content>. Weight rising with treatment and supplements. [S]<list><item>Continue the dietitian&apos;s plan.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Symptom review: cough, sputum, hemoptysis, fever, night sweats</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Occasional cough; no fever, sweats, or blood</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Weight, with the pyrazinamide dose checked against the weight band</td><td>Monthly</td><td>Completed today</td><td>49.6 kg; pyrazinamide band unchanged</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Adverse effects: hepatitis symptoms, tendon pain, numbness or tingling, rash, nausea, orange body fluids</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Early nausea resolved; no tendon pain, tingling, rash, or jaundice</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Liver tests (ALT, AST, bilirubin)</td><td>Baseline; repeat for symptoms or risk factors (here at two to three weeks, for low weight and albumin)</td><td>Completed today</td><td>ALT 34, AST 31 U/L, bilirubin 0.8 mg/dL, all normal</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Sputum acid-fast smear and culture</td><td>Baseline, then monthly until two consecutive cultures are negative</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Three smears in the third week negative; baseline cultures growing M. tuberculosis complex; third-week cultures pending</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Rapid molecular testing for isoniazid and fluoroquinolone resistance</td><td>At baseline</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No isoniazid (katG, inhA) or fluoroquinolone (gyrA, gyrB) resistance mutations</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Phenotypic susceptibility (rifampin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, moxifloxacin)</td><td>At baseline</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Set up on the baseline isolate; pending</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Doses observed and the completion clock (56 doses within 70 days, 63 within 84 days, 119 within five months)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Nineteen of nineteen doses observed</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Home airborne isolation and the release criteria</td><td>Until three negative smears, clinical improvement, and two weeks of treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Release criteria met; released by the health department two days ago</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Contact investigation (students, friends, close contacts)</td><td>Led by the health department from diagnosis</td><td>Referred</td><td>In progress with the health department; no results in his record</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Nutrition: dietitian, intake, supplement drinks</td><td>Every visit until weight is restored</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Two dietitian visits; two supplement drinks a day, apart from the dose</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Sweat chloride and serum alpha-1 antitrypsin</td><td>Once, to address the alternatives</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Sweat chloride 18 mmol/L; alpha-1 antitrypsin 142 mg/dL; both normal</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression screen (PHQ-9)</td><td>At diagnosis, at two months, and at the end of treatment</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Next at two months; mood better today</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Complete all 119 doses within five months by video-observed therapy. In progress; target 1838-07-21 [S]</item><item>Sputum culture negative at two months. In progress; target 1838-07-21 [S]</item><item>Return to teaching once the health department confirms he is no longer infectious. Outcome by 1838-04-07: Released from isolation after three negative smears and more than two weeks of treatment; teaching again from the third week [S]</item><item>Weight back to at least 55 kg by the end of treatment. In progress; target 1838-07-21 [S]</item><item>PHQ-9 below 5 by the end of treatment. In progress; target 1838-07-21 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Official American Thoracic Society/Centers for Disease Control and Prevention/Infectious Diseases Society of America Clinical Practice Guidelines: Treatment of Drug-Susceptible Tuberculosis (2016); Interim Guidance: 4-Month Rifapentine-Moxifloxacin Regimen for the Treatment of Drug-Susceptible Pulmonary Tuberculosis - United States, 2022 (2022); An official ATS statement: hepatotoxicity of antituberculosis therapy (2006); Guidelines for the investigation of contacts of persons with infectious tuberculosis: recommendations from the National Tuberculosis Controllers Association and CDC (2005); Diagnosis of Cystic Fibrosis: Consensus Guidelines from the Cystic Fibrosis Foundation (2017); American Thoracic Society/European Respiratory Society statement: standards for the diagnosis and management of individuals with alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency (2003); Consensus statement: Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics and American Society for Parenteral and Enteral Nutrition: characteristics recommended for the identification and documentation of adult malnutrition (undernutrition) (2012)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: End-of-intensive-phase review in about five weeks, with the two-month sputum culture, liver tests, a blood count, and the PHQ-9. Next scheduled visit 1838-05-15: end of the eight-week intensive phase: susceptibility results, the two-month sputum culture, and the start of the continuation phase. [S]</paragraph></text>

